{
  "schema": "ld-s10y-image/figure-spec@2",
  "id": "fig-236",
  "mode": "deterministic",
  "description": "Two independent parallel-wire circuits: left switch open, no steady left current; right closed with a single sustained current. There is no upper interconnection and no dashed reference. One added right current arrow is an illustrative direction convention, not a source-pixel direction claim.",
  "source": {
    "image": {
      "path": "/Users/yong/.codex/worktrees/00b1/lemmadeck/ssot-resources/soviet10year-textbooks/artifacts/9p/figures/fig-236.png",
      "sha256": "fb68d4715ed4ee3fe04ab7e80c0c9e7186adaed76bbc54d322311f88b1fcff36"
    },
    "authoritativeText": [
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s5-n114",
        "text": "静止的电荷之间有电相互作用力，这种力由库仑定律来确定。\n每个电荷周围都有电场。\n一个电荷的电场对另一个电荷施加力，另一个电荷的电场也对前一个电荷施加力。\n\n用电场来说明这种相互作用，就是原书所说的近距作用观点。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s5-n114",
        "text": "电荷运动形成电流时，还会表现出磁相互作用。\n下面的实验把这种作用与前面所说的静电荷之间的电相互作用区分开来。\n取两根柔软的导体，把它们竖直放好，分别接在电源的两极上（图 233）。\n\n它们的另外一端没有连接，电路还未闭合，导体中没有持续电流；\n此时看不到明显的相吸或相斥。\n用导线把另外一端连接起来，使电路闭合，两根导体中的电流方向相反（图234）。\n\n在这个实验中，相邻、平行的通电部分彼此排斥。\n把电路接成两根导体中的电流方向相同的情况（图 235），相邻、平行的通电部分就彼此吸引。\n这里比较的是实验中这些平行部分的相互作用，不是说任意形状的整条电路都只由电流方向决定受力。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s5-n114",
        "text": "图 233"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s5-n114",
        "text": "图 234"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s5-n114",
        "text": "图 235"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s5-n114",
        "text": "在七年级物理课程中，已经学过磁现象与电荷的运动有关；\n电流就是电荷的定向运动。\n因此，这个实验里通电导体之间表现的是**磁相互作用**。\n\n通电导体在这种相互作用中受到的力叫做**磁力**。\n它与静止电荷之间的电相互作用要区分开来：这里的磁作用随导体中的电流情况而改变。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s5-n114",
        "text": "保持实验中导体的摆放方式不变，只把其中一根导体的电流方向反过来，原来的相吸就变成相斥，原来的相斥就变成相吸。\n也就是说，反转其中一根导体的电流，会反转这两段平行导体之间磁力的方向。\n如果只有一根导体中有电流，另一根导体中没有电流，就看不到前面那种通电导体之间的相吸或相斥（图 236）。\n\n有电流的导体仍有磁作用，但另一根导体没有持续电流，所以在这里描述的实验中，不再有两股持续电流之间相应的磁相互作用；\n这不是说没有电流的物体在任何情况下都不受任何力。\n图 237 中，其中一根导体换成了由两根导线拧在一起组成的导线对。\n\n两根导线靠得很近，其中的电流大小相同、方向相反。\n在导线对外部，离导线对的距离比两根导线的间距大得多时，两股电流产生的磁作用大致相反，会大部分抵消。\n所以，在这个实验中，导线对与另一根导体之间看不到明显的磁相互作用。\n\n放在导线对附近的磁针，如果它离导线的距离仍比两根导线的间距大得多，也没有明显偏转。\n这就是原书用导线对说明的外部磁作用近似抵消。\n这里的“抵消”是说外部合成的磁作用很小，并不是每根导线都不产生磁场，也不是所有位置的磁场都完全为零。\n\n靠近单根导线时，不能把导线对当成处处没有磁场。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s5-n114",
        "text": "图 236"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s5-n114",
        "text": "图 237"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s5-n114",
        "text": "Stationary charges exert electric forces on each other, and Coulomb's law determines these forces. Every charge has an electric field around it.\n\nThe field of one charge exerts a force on the other charge, and the other charge's field exerts a force on the first. Explaining the interaction through fields is what the source calls the local-action viewpoint."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s5-n114",
        "text": "When charges move to form a current, magnetic interaction also appears. The following experiment distinguishes this interaction from the electric interaction of stationary charges described above.\n\nTake two flexible conducting wires, place them vertically, and connect them to opposite terminals of a power supply (Figure 233). Their other ends are not connected, so the circuit is open and no steady current flows in the wires;\n\nno clear attraction or repulsion is observed.\n\nConnect the other ends with a wire to close the circuit, so the currents in the two wires run in opposite directions (Figure 234). In this experiment, the adjacent, parallel current-carrying portions repel each other.\n\nConnect the circuit so that the currents in the two wires run in the same direction (Figure 235), and the adjacent, parallel current-carrying portions attract each other. This comparison concerns those parallel portions in the experiment;\n\nit does not say that current direction alone determines the force on every possible shape of complete circuit."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s5-n114",
        "text": "Figure 233"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s5-n114",
        "text": "Figure 234"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s5-n114",
        "text": "Figure 235"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s5-n114",
        "text": "In the seventh-grade physics course, you learned that magnetic phenomena are related to the motion of charges;\n\nan electric current is directed motion of charges. The interaction between the current-carrying wires in this experiment is therefore **magnetic interaction**.\n\nThe force on a current-carrying wire in this interaction is called **magnetic force**. Distinguish it from the electric interaction of stationary charges: the magnetic interaction here changes with the currents in the wires."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s5-n114",
        "text": "Keep the wires arranged as in the experiment and reverse the current in just one wire: attraction becomes repulsion, and repulsion becomes attraction. Reversing the current in one wire reverses the direction of the magnetic force between these parallel portions.\n\nIf current flows in only one wire and not in the other, the attraction or repulsion seen between the current-carrying wires is no longer observed (Figure 236). The wire carrying current still has a magnetic effect, but the other wire has no steady current, so the corresponding magnetic interaction between two steady currents is absent in the experiment described here;\n\nthis does not mean that an object without current can never experience any force.\n\nIn Figure 237, one of the conducting wires is replaced by a pair of wires twisted together. The two wires are close together and carry currents of equal magnitude in opposite directions.\n\nOutside the pair, at distances much greater than the separation of the wires, the magnetic effects of the two currents are approximately opposite and largely cancel. Thus, no clear magnetic interaction between the pair and the other wire is observed in this experiment.\n\nA compass near the pair also shows no clear deflection if its distance from the wires is still much greater than their separation. This is the approximate cancellation of the external magnetic effect illustrated by the source's wire pair.\n\nHere, cancellation means that the combined external magnetic effect is small;\n\nit does not mean that each wire produces no magnetic field or that the field is exactly zero everywhere. Near an individual wire, the pair must not be treated as having no magnetic field anywhere."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s5-n114",
        "text": "Figure 236"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s5-n114",
        "text": "Figure 237"
      }
